@user1:
This should be deleted.

@author:
Done.

@user1:
The leading `\n` here causes an excess newline when there are mixed HTTP/DNS challenges. It could be avoided by making it conditional on there being any `http01_achalls`.


@author:
This would only be an issue when Certbot actually would support mixed challenge types. Is that the case?

@user1:
    certbot certonly --manual -d example.com -d "*.example.com" --manual-auth-hook /bin/true --debug-challenges

is one way to produce a mixed challenge type scenario.

@author:
Ah, cool, I did not know that. I thought the challenge type function would prefer having all challenges of the same type when I looked at the code, but perhaps it gives priority to what preference the authenticator has.

Anyway, fixed the newline issue without requiring it to be conditional :slightly_smiling_face:

@author:
I have tried this approach, as we discussed, but this doesn't seem possible.
The test fail at the local ``conan source`` command, while trying to execute this, because it doesn't have enough information to construct the ``buildenv``, the ``conan source`` command do not construct the graph or have dependencies at all. Inhibiting this so it doesn't do anything and it is no-op when it is a local ``conan source`` command is not straightforward at all.



@user1:
This was my fear when first proposed. Do you have any preferred fallback for this case?

@user1:
Are you sure these quotes are to be removed? They can be very necessary when there are path containing spaces, can you please try to keep them?

@author:
It was causing cmake warnings previously. But you’re right, there is perhaps another approach to fix those. 

@author:
You already found the fix apparently :D

@user1:
That fact that this is only the `remote.name` might hamper users from coming up with useful solutions, and they have no (easy) way to instantiate the proper `conan_api` either to re-resolve it. Maybe we can check if passing the whole `Remote` object would be better?

@user2:
Yes, using the remote ``url`` for example might be useful for users.

@user1:
I am a bit concerned with the order `'bhilq'`. Which return type is chosen when `argmin(..., dtype=None, ...)` is called? I thought we'd need to put `'q'` as the leading character to match NumPy default, but I could be wrong. (I don't have access now to test this PR.)

@author:
Thank you for pointing out! It seems you are correct. Some of the tests were failing.
Fixed in eb90e95d5e3a421d31a56c2eaec4d32a0471780d.

@user1:
Is it possible that here `shape` will be with outside == True? Thus we will insert the shape but it looks wrong.

@author:
added filter to remove starting shapes with outside==True after processing

